# Managing relational schema and R2RML

AshR2ML does not manage a database schema itself. The Ash data layer—normally AshPostgres—continues to own relational persistence and migrations.

AshR2ML manages the **semantic correspondence** between that relational schema and the virtual RDF graph exposed through R2RML.

```text
Ash.Resource
    │
    ├── AshPostgres → Ecto migration → PostgreSQL
    │
    └── AshR2ML → R2RML mapping → virtual RDF
```

The two branches must describe the same admitted subject.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ash_postgres, "~> 2.0"},
    {:ash_r2ml, "~> 1.0"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## Define the resource once

```elixir
defmodule Schema.Account do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Schema.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshR2ML.Resource]

  postgres do
    table "accounts"
    repo Schema.Repo
  end

  r2rml do
    class "https://example.org/Account"

    subject do
      template "https://example.org/accounts/{id}"
      term_type :iri
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :account_number, :string do
      allow_nil? false
      public? true

      rdf do
        predicate "https://example.org/accountNumber"
      end
    end

    attribute :balance, :decimal do
      allow_nil? false
      public? true

      rdf do
        predicate "https://example.org/balance"
      end
    end
  end

  identities do
    identity :account_number, [:account_number]
  end
end
```

AshPostgres derives and enforces the relational structure. AshR2ML derives the semantic mapping.

## Generate relational migrations with AshPostgres

Use the normal AshPostgres migration workflow for the application.

Conceptually:

```bash
mix ash.codegen create_accounts
mix ash.migrate
```

or the application's documented AshPostgres migration commands.

AshR2ML deliberately does not duplicate this functionality.

The resulting relational schema is expected to contain structures equivalent to:

```sql
CREATE TABLE accounts (
  id uuid PRIMARY KEY,
  account_number text NOT NULL,
  balance numeric NOT NULL
);

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX accounts_account_number_index
  ON accounts(account_number);
```

The exact SQL remains the responsibility of the relational data layer and migration system.

## Generate the semantic mapping

```elixir
{:ok, ttl} = AshR2ML.R2RML.render([Schema.Account])
File.write!("priv/r2rml/schema.ttl", ttl)
```

The mapping is equivalent to:

```turtle
<#Account>
    a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rr:logicalTable [ rr:tableName "accounts" ] ;
    rr:subjectMap [
        rr:template "https://example.org/accounts/{id}" ;
        rr:class <https://example.org/Account> ;
        rr:termType rr:IRI
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate <https://example.org/accountNumber> ;
        rr:objectMap [
            rr:column "account_number" ;
            rr:datatype xsd:string
        ]
    ] ;
    rr:predicateObjectMap [
        rr:predicate <https://example.org/balance> ;
        rr:objectMap [
            rr:column "balance" ;
            rr:datatype xsd:decimal
        ]
    ] .
```

No table or column is re-declared by hand in the semantic layer when AshR2ML can derive it from the resource/data layer.

## Add a relational relationship

```elixir
defmodule Schema.Transaction do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Schema.Domain,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshR2ML.Resource]

  postgres do
    table "transactions"
    repo Schema.Repo
  end

  r2rml do
    class "https://example.org/Transaction"

    subject do
      template "https://example.org/transactions/{id}"
      term_type :iri
    end
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :amount, :decimal do
      allow_nil? false

      rdf do
        predicate "https://example.org/amount"
      end
    end
  end

  relationships do
    belongs_to :account, Schema.Account do
      allow_nil? false

      rdf do
        predicate "https://example.org/account"
      end
    end
  end
end
```

The relational schema owns the foreign key:

```text
transactions.account_id → accounts.id
```

AshR2ML converts that relationship to an R2RML reference object map:

```turtle
rr:predicateObjectMap [
    rr:predicate <https://example.org/account> ;
    rr:objectMap [
        rr:parentTriplesMap <#Account> ;
        rr:joinCondition [
            rr:child "account_id" ;
            rr:parent "id"
        ]
    ]
] .
```

## Schema changes

A schema change may affect both relational storage and semantic projection.

### Relational-only rename with stable semantics

Suppose the physical column changes from:

```text
account_number
```

to:

```text
external_account_number
```

while the RDF predicate remains:

```text
https://example.org/accountNumber
```

Update the Ash resource/data-layer source configuration and generate the migration. Do not change the RDF predicate merely because the physical column changed.

This is one reason semantic identity and predicates are independent from storage names.

### Semantic predicate change

If the domain meaning genuinely changes and the application adopts a new predicate IRI, update the semantic DSL/application profile and regenerate R2RML.

The relational table may remain unchanged.

### Relationship ownership change

Moving a foreign key or replacing a join table changes the relational path. AshR2ML must re-introspect the Ash relationship and regenerate the corresponding `rr:joinCondition`.

Do not preserve a stale hand-authored join in R2RML.

## Validate relational correspondence

AshR2ML verifies that mapping references correspond to known Ash/data-layer structure before rendering.

```elixir
:ok = AshR2ML.verify([
  Schema.Account,
  Schema.Transaction
])
```

Verification includes:

- logical table is resolvable,
- mapped attributes have source columns or supported expressions,
- subject-template fields exist,
- relationship source/destination attributes resolve,
- destination resource has a subject map,
- join columns are compatible with the referenced identity,
- RDF datatypes are lawful for the Ash value types.

## Validate R2RML syntax

Generated Turtle is parsed as RDF as part of the conformance suite.

A mapping that renders text but cannot be parsed as RDF is a failed build, not a warning.

## Validate with a real OBDA engine

The strongest integration test runs the generated mapping against the real relational schema.

```text
fixtures inserted through Ash
       ↓
PostgreSQL
       ↓
generated R2RML
       ↓
OBDA engine
       ↓
SPARQL results
```

For example:

```sparql
SELECT ?transaction ?account ?amount
WHERE {
  ?transaction a <https://example.org/Transaction> ;
               <https://example.org/account> ?account ;
               <https://example.org/amount> ?amount .
}
```

The returned `?transaction` and `?account` IRIs must be the subject IRIs manufactured from the same rows Ash observes.

## Ontology-first schema management

When resources are generated from RDF/SHACL with ggen, the operational source hierarchy is:

```text
ontology/profile/SHACL
      ↓
ggen
      ↓
Ash resources
      ↓
AshPostgres migrations
      +
AshR2ML R2RML
```

Do not modify the generated Ash resource to repair a migration or mapping. Repair the application profile, SHACL shape, ggen query/template, or compiler logic and regenerate.

## Drift detection

There are three distinct drift classes.

### Generated-source drift

Generated Ash differs from what ggen would produce.

Detect by regeneration and a clean diff check.

### Relational drift

The live PostgreSQL schema differs from the migrations/resource metadata expected by Ash.

Detect with the application's normal AshPostgres/Ecto migration verification.

### Semantic drift

Generated R2RML no longer corresponds to the current Ash relationship, source column, datatype, or subject identity.

Detect with `AshR2ML.verify/1`, deterministic regeneration, and the OBDA integration suite.

## Release artifact

A deployment that exposes SPARQL should publish the generated mapping alongside the application release, for example:

```text
priv/r2rml/application.ttl
```

The artifact should be generated from the same source revision used to build the application.

A receipt can bind:

```text
application commit
Ash resource digest
migration digest
AshR2ML mapping digest
R2RML artifact digest
```

so an operator can prove which semantic graph projection corresponds to which deployed relational schema.

## Operational rule

Never treat R2RML as a second schema authority.

The authority chain is:

```text
Ash / ontology-first source
      ↓
relational schema + semantic mapping
      ↓
PostgreSQL + virtual RDF
```

If the relational and semantic projections disagree, fix the authoritative model and regenerate both projections.
